1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:06,740
Hello and welcome to this massive open online course, Introduction to BioRisk Management.

2
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:12,660
I am your instructor Kenneth, and in this segment we will be discussing about samples

3
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:18,580
and specifically referring to hospital derived samples.

4
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:25,500
Now in your role as a laboratory technician in a biosafety level 3 laboratory, you will

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,300
be required to analyze samples from different locations.

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:36,420
Samples which are derived from researchers which consist of exonic opeocultures are easy

7
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:39,860
to analyze because the risk can be assessed very easily.

8
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:45,060
We are aware of the biological agent and we are also aware of any modifications which have

9
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:47,900
been made to that biological agent.

10
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:53,180
The situation changes slightly when we have to analyze samples derived from patients.

11
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:58,340
These may be in the form of swabs, serum samples, blood, urine or stools.

12
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:05,100
When samples are received from hospitals, they may consist of more than one biological agent

13
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,460
within a single sample.

14
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:11,100
How do we address this risk?

15
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:16,060
In the case of hospital samples, we assume the highest possible risk.

16
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:22,260
When samples are received from hospitals, they will be shipped in a special packing container.

17
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:27,220
This is an example of a bag in which the sample will be shipped.

18
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:36,180
This bag will be sealed, it will be locked with a tie and then shipped in a container

19
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:45,340
which may contain ice or some other refrigerant such as carbon dioxide, CO2 ice, what we call

20
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:47,420
as the dry ice.

21
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:50,940
When you receive these samples, the first thing which we need to do is to ensure that

22
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:56,380
the outer packing material is thoroughly sterilized with a sterilant.

23
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:02,780
In our case, we use 70% ethanol, we wait for around 30 seconds and we open the bag after

24
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:03,780
30 seconds.

25
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:08,660
The 30 second period is known as the contact time.

26
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:14,180
Once you have opened your bag, you will obtain the sample.

27
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:20,660
I am going to take out a sample from this bag.

28
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:25,580
The sample will be generally shipped in a tube like this.

29
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,580
This is a sample containing tube.

30
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:35,220
I cannot reveal the details of the patient as this is a patient derived sample.

31
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:40,900
This sample is basically a serum sample you can see in the tube.

32
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:48,340
You can note that there is a small ward of bandage over here.

33
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:52,940
There is a purpose for this specific ward of bandage here.

34
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:59,980
When I open the sample in the biological safety cabinet, any of the serum which drips from

35
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:04,020
this particular tube will be trapped by this ward.

36
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:08,420
It will not spill onto my gloves and cause a breach of containment.

37
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:12,580
This is one of the key aspects of transporting samples from hospitals.

38
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:17,980
They must be accompanied with the proper transport security.

39
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:23,420
The technician who has drawn the sample or the doctor at the hospital has to ensure that

40
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:29,860
the sample is properly labeled and properly bagged so as to ensure that the transport

41
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:34,500
agent or the one who transport the sample to the hospital as well as the technician

42
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:41,940
who works in the laboratory is basically protected from the biological agent.

43
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:46,740
What happens when these samples come into our lab?

44
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:48,820
Basically there are two types of samples which come in.

45
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:51,980
The first in which we have to culture the biological agent.

46
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:59,620
In this case, we practice the extreme set of precautions and we assess the risk in terms

47
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:00,620
of a live culture.

48
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:06,020
So, the sample, for instance, bacterial samples may need to be transferred onto media.

49
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:10,580
In the case of serum samples which are to be analyzed for instance, nucleic acids

50
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:15,940
or in this case, we are analyzing samples from COVID-19 patients or suspected patients.

51
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:20,180
We move on to the next step which is inactivation of the sample.

52
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:24,420
Now, for inactivation of the samples, we have to follow the standard operating procedure

53
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:26,460
for that particular biological agent.

54
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:34,660
In this case of COVID-19, it involves elevating the sample to a temperature of 60 to 70 degrees

55
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:41,700
in a thermal block and after a period of one hour, the sample is basically inactivated.

56
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:47,820
Now, remember that all these procedures must be conducted within a biological safety cabinet

57
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:53,300
with the appropriate PPEs and in accordance with the administrative procedures

58
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,580
or administrative controls.

59
00:04:55,580 --> 00:05:01,260
Once the sample is inactivated, we can process to the next step which is the extraction

60
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:06,540
of the nucleic acid which must also be conducted in a biological safety cabinet.

61
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:15,540
Once the nucleic acid is extracted, the sample is ready for processing which in terms of the analysis.

62
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:22,060
So, in our case for COVID-19, we use a thermal cycler, a real-time thermal cycler

63
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:27,980
which will analyze the sample for the specific biological agent.

64
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:34,820
Now, all these aspects need to be integrated into your sample preparation strategy.

65
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:38,580
You must assess the risk of the procedure itself.

66
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:43,100
So, coming back to what we have learned, we have a risk assessment.

67
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:45,980
So, we assess the risk caused by the biological agent.

68
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:51,380
In the case of a sample from hospital patients, we assume the highest risk.

69
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:53,900
We then apply the specific controls.

70
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:57,100
So, in this case, I'm in a biological safety level 3 laboratory

71
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:01,580
and I'm applying the pertinent engineering controls as well

72
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:06,300
as the personal protective equipment which I'm wearing and the engineering controls

73
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:09,100
which is this unit itself.

74
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:12,260
These are some of the key points which we need to take into consideration

75
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:20,700
when managing biological agents where from known as well as unknown samples.

76
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:25,260
Thank you very much for watching this short segment.

77
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:28,100
I will discuss further in my lecture.

78
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:30,940
In the meantime, stay bio-safe.

79
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:31,500
Thank you.
